++`budget_usd` is documented as a hard cost ceiling, but a **cold-start** run can
++exceed it before the runner has any prior cost data. `BenchmarkSet.budget_usd`
++is a "Hard cost ceiling" (`benchmark.py:64`) and `run_benchmark` advertises a
++budget abort before a pending dispatch would exceed it (`benchmark.py:567-569`),
++but the check coerces `estimated_next` to `0.0` when there are no prior
++Claude-cost records (`benchmark.py:591-614`). So a first paid invocation with
++`budget_usd = 0.01` and a Claude-backed triple that costs `$0.50` **dispatches
++anyway** and only considers aborting on the *next* run. The "hard ceiling" is
++non-enforcing for the highest-risk case — the first paid benchmark run. Cost
++overrun is the #1 risk the #146 design flagged; this is a real safety-boundary
++gap.
